<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<task id="062" project="spaarke-auth-v4-dataverse-MI">
  <metadata>
    <title>Startup assertion: no secret-backed BFF identity outside Development (FR-F3)</title>
    <phase>6 Forcing Functions</phase>
    <gate>022 complete</gate>
    <status>not-started</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>auth tag, modifies .cs on the startup path; a wrong implementation prevents boot.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>sonnet</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Bounded runtime guard.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <parallel-group>G</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>true</parallel-safe>
    <deps>022</deps>
    <tags>auth,bff-api,startup,forcing-function</tags>
    <estimated-effort>2-3 hours</estimated-effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Outside the Development environment, fail fast at startup if any BFF-identity credential resolves to a client secret once the migration is complete — rather than silently degrading to the fallback and appearing healthy.</prompt>
  <role>SPAARKE platform developer.</role>
  <goal>A non-Development startup guard that makes secret-backed BFF identity a loud failure rather than a silent regression.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>The ordered credential selector (task 021) falls through on purpose, which is correct during the migration and dangerous after it: a broken MI-FIC would silently resolve to the secret and look healthy. This guard closes that window once task 033 has removed the secret.</background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file role="modify">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/Auth/ManagedIdentityAssertionProvider.cs</file>
      <file role="modify">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Program.cs</file>
    </relevant-files>
    <dependencies><dependency task="022" status="pending">Migrated clients</dependency></dependencies>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: Development is EXEMPT — local dev legitimately uses a user-secret fallback for OBO.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: the guard must be inert until task 033 completes, or it blocks the very rollout it protects. Gate it on configuration, not on a hardcoded date.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: fail fast at STARTUP, not at first token request — a deferred failure is what AgentTokenOptions does today and it hides the problem until traffic arrives.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: do NOT use eager connection or ValidateOnBuild — that is the #3b SIGABRT failure mode.</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <knowledge>
    <topic>Startup validation, fail-fast guards</topic>
    <files>
      <file>.claude/adr/ADR-028-spaarke-auth-architecture.md</file>
      <file>.claude/adr/ADR-010-di-minimalism.md</file>
      <file>projects/spaarke-auth-v4-dataverse-MI/notes/CREDENTIAL-INVENTORY.md</file>
    </files>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step>Add a configuration-gated startup check for the resolved BFF-identity credential type.</step>
    <step>Exempt Development.</step>
    <step>Fail with a message naming the offending credential and the expected one.</step>
    <step>Verify the guard is inert while the secret is still the intentional fallback.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>The guard cannot determine the resolved credential type without performing a token acquisition at startup — STOP; that reintroduces the eager-connect crash risk.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <outputs>
    <output type="modify">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/Auth/ManagedIdentityAssertionProvider.cs</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion>With the guard enabled outside Development and a secret-backed credential resolved, startup fails with an actionable message.</criterion>
    <criterion>Development is unaffected.</criterion>
    <criterion>Negative case: while the secret is still the intentional fallback (pre-033), the guard is inert and the app boots.</criterion>
    <criterion>Negative case: no eager connection or ValidateOnBuild is introduced — no SIGABRT-class startup risk.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>
</task>
